    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d279">

                <pb facs="0225" n="213" xml:id="pg213"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.Consultation(2)/234: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Uruguay</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Montevideo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-07-09T12:00:00-03:30">July 9, 1940—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received
                        2:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>153. A few days ago the French Minister, under instructions from his Government,
                    informed Guani<anchor n="44" xml:id="d279-f1"/> that the French Government saw
                    no reason why the American States should discuss at Habana the question of
                    transfer of territory in the Western Hemisphere from one non-American Power to
                    another, adding that the German Government had never raised this question.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d279-f1" n="44" xml:id="d279-f1-content">
                <p>Alberto Guani, Uruguayan Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
